    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d146">

                <pb facs="0292" n="160" xml:id="pg160"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source"> Truman Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 146 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The President’s Adviser and Assistant</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hopkins</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the President
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d146-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]<anchor n="2" xml:id="d146-f2"/></seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">operational priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-30">30 May 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Personal and top secret for the eyes of the President only from Hopkins.</p>
                <p> Stalin is very anxious to discuss problems concerning Japan at his following
                        [<hi rendition="#italic">forthcoming</hi>] conference with you.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <list>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item> Certain elements in Japan are putting out peace feelers[.] Therefore we
                        should consider together our joint attitude and act in concert about the
                        surrender of Japan. …</item>
                    <label/>
                    <item rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item> Stalin expects that Russia will share in the actual occupation of Japan
                        and wants an agreement with us and the British as to zones of
                        occupation.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item> Stalin also wants an understanding between the Allies as to areas of
                        operation in Manchuria and China.</item>
                </list>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d146-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d146-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Naval Attaché, Moscow, via Navy channels. Cf. <ref
                        target="#d26">document No. 26</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d146-f2" n="2" xml:id="d146-f2-content">
                <p>For a fuller text of this message, see “The Entry of the Soviet Union Into the
                    War Against Japan: Military Plans, 1941–1945” (Washington, Department of
                    Defense, processed, 1955), p. 73.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
